<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Musta'sim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1242">1242</a>–<a href="/years/1258">1258</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as al-Mustaʿsim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Musta'sim was the last Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> to rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. When Mongol forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu Khan</a> captured the city in February 1258, he was executed and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a> was destroyed. His death ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> of Baghdad, which had ruled since 750, and cleared the way for the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-mustasim.jpg" alt="al-Musta'sim" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DrFO.Jr.Tn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_Abbasside_-_al-Musta%27sim_bi-llah_-_641_AH_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Ahmad Abdallah was born around 1213, a son of the Abbasid caliph al-Mustansir and his Abyssinian concubine Hajir. After his father's death he succeeded to the throne in late 1242 as al-Musta'sim billah, and he imprisoned several members of the Abbasid family who might rival him, among them his uncle Ahmad ibn al-Zahir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1249 the Ayyubid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> as-Salih Ayyub died during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seventh-crusade">Seventh Crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> troops proclaimed his widow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shajar-al-durr">Shajar al-Durr</a> sultan. Al-Musta'sim refused to recognise her because of her gender, reportedly sending the message that if the Mamluks had no men, he would send them some. Since an Ayyubid sultan gained legitimacy only through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s recognition, this was a setback for the Mamluks, who installed their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aybak">Aybak</a>. Al-Musta'sim's mediation later helped bring an agreement giving the Mamluks southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a>.</p><p>The Mongol empire had meanwhile come to border his state. In 1255 Hulegu Khan forced him to lend troops for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alamut">Alamut</a> campaign, and in 1258 the khagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> ordered Hulegu to subjugate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>. Al-Musta'sim refused the terms, largely on the advice of his grand vizier Ibn al-Alkami, who seems to have assured him that the Islamic world would come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>'s aid. He raised 20,000 ill-equipped and poorly disciplined cavalry, but had lost the loyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By 11 January 1258 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> held both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. They breached its dikes on 29 January, trapped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s cavalry and took much of the defences by 5 February with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a>. Hulegu refused negotiation and about 3,000 notables sent to treat were murdered. The city surrendered on 10 February, and three days later the Mongols entered and began a week of massacre and destruction, looting mosques, palaces, libraries and hospitals. Al-Musta'sim was forced to watch and was executed on 20 February, according to most accounts wrapped in a rug and trampled by horses.</p><p>His death ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> as a political and religious entity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> later installed members of his family as caliphs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, a line of symbolic figures that lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-conquest-of-egypt">Ottoman conquest of Egypt</a> in 1517. For almost a century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> kept putting his name on their coins.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Musta'sim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hulagu Khan's forces took the city in February 1258, executed Caliph <b>al-Musta'sim</b>, and sacked its libraries, including the House of Wisdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1258 Mongol forces captured Baghdad and executed the Abbasid caliph <b>al-Mustaʿsim</b>, ending the Abbasid Caliphate of Baghdad that had ruled since 750.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After a short siege, the Abbasid Caliph <b>al-Musta'sim</b> surrendered Baghdad to Hulagu Khan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1250" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of al-Musta'sim"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6016 4653h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6016 4653h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=1250">What was here in 1250? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mustansir-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mustansir-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Al-Mustansir I</span><span class="tp">Father &#183; 1192–1242</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Who is this Holagou that dares to rise against them? If he be desirous of peace, let him instantly depart from the sacred territory; and perhaps he may obtain from our clemency the pardon of his fault.&#8221;</p><footer>Answer to the envoys of Hulagu, 1257 (in Gibbon, Decline and Fall, chapter 64) &#183; tr. Edward Gibbon, 1788 &#183; as Gibbon gives it; &quot;them&quot; are the Abbasid caliphs, whose throne he said rested on the divine decree; Hulagu took Baghdad and put him to death in 1258 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/736/pg736-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
